<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Nabhani dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1154 CE – 1624 CE (approx.) · capital Bahla</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A line of kings who ruled the Omani interior from Bahla for nearly five centuries between periods of Ibadi imamate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Nabhan, an Arab tribe of the Azd, took power in inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> in the mid-12th century, as the Ibadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> weakened. Ruling as maliks, or kings, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahla">Bahla</a> and its great fort, they governed the interior while the coast was dominated by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ormus">Hormuz</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Unlike elected Ibadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>, the Nabhanis ruled by hereditary right, and Ibadi scholars often condemned them as tyrants. Their power waxed and waned; elected imams sometimes ruled alongside or instead of them from the 15th century. Their era is poorly documented, but the poet-king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayman-ibn-sulayman-al-nabhani">Sulayman ibn Sulayman al-Nabhani</a> is remembered, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahla">Bahla's</a> walls and fort date largely from this period.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 16th century the Portuguese took the coast, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-muscat">Portuguese Muscat</a>. In 1624 Ibadi scholars elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-ibn-murshid">Nasir ibn Murshid</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yaaruba-dynasty">Ya'aruba dynasty</a>, which reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and ended Nabhani rule.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1154 CE – 1624 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50"